<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>An aircraft flies along the equator from <m>37^\circ\,\mathrm{E}</m> to <m>10^\circ\,\mathrm{E}</m> at <m>{360}</m> knots. It leaves at <m>0{7}:{30}</m> local time.</p><p>(a) Find the flight time, to the nearest minute.</p><p><fillin characters="3" name="ans1"/> h <fillin characters="3" name="ans2"/> min   </p><p>(b) Find the local time on arrival, to the nearest minute. Use the 24-hour clock.</p><p><fillin characters="3" name="ans3"/> : <fillin characters="3" name="ans4"/>   </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) The difference in longitude is <m>{27}^\circ = {1620}'</m>, so the distance along the equator is <m>{1620}</m> nm. Time <m>= {1620} \div {360} = {4.500}</m> h <m>= {4}</m> h <m>{30}</m> min.</p><p>(b) By the clock of the starting place the aircraft lands at <m>{12}:0{0}</m>. It flew west through <m>{27}^\circ</m>, so local time is <m>{108}</m> minutes behind: it arrives at <m>{10}:{12}</m> local time.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>4</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>30</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>10</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>12</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>